But how do we ensure there are no cycles? If we have n nodes and n-1 edges, and each node except the root has in-degree 1, and the root has in-degree 0, then there are no cycles (since a cycle would require all nodes in the cycle to have in-degree at least 1, but the sum of in-degrees is n-1, which is exactly the number of edges, so there can't be any "extra" edges to form a cycle).

Actually, in a directed graph with n nodes and n-1 edges, if one node has in-degree 0 and all others have in-degree 1, then there are no cycles. This is because a cycle would require at least as many edges as nodes in the cycle, but we only have n-1 edges total.

So the approach is correct.

